# c.bot -- the "ffi::c" namespace: the C ABI value universe (C-ABI-TYPES.md).
#
# These are the values a C boundary carries, modeled as C's ABI sees them,
# not as C's source language spells them. Nothing here calls C yet: there
# is no `extern c fn`, no linking and no callback (C-ABI-TYPES.md, "What is
# not here").
#
# C integer scalars (intrinsic types of this namespace):
#
#   ffi::c::Char ffi::c::SChar ffi::c::UChar ffi::c::Short ffi::c::UShort
#   ffi::c::Int ffi::c::UInt ffi::c::Long ffi::c::ULong
#   ffi::c::LongLong ffi::c::ULongLong
#
# Each is an integer domain: its values are ordinary Ints in exactly the
# compilation's C target's range of that C type (`-target`, the host's by
# default: ffi::c::Long is Int in -2^63..2^63-1 on linux-x86_64 and
# -2^31..2^31-1 on windows-x86_64; ffi::c::Char is signed or unsigned as the
# target's plain char is). Arithmetic on them is Botlish arithmetic (no C
# promotion, no wrapping); an Int becomes one only where its range proves it
# fits. ffi::c::Int and ffi::c::Long stay two types where their ranges agree.
#
# C floating types (intrinsic types of this namespace,
# C-FLOAT-FFI-MILESTONE-1.md): C representations whose values are Float64s.
#
#   ffi::c::Double            C's double, IEEE 754 binary64 (8 bytes): every
#                             Float64 is one, exactly
#   ffi::c::Float             C's float, IEEE 754 binary32 (4 bytes): a
#                             Float64 leaving for one is rounded to nearest,
#                             ties to even, and one that rounds to an
#                             infinity is NonFiniteFloat before C runs (never
#                             clamped); a float entering widens exactly
#
# They name C types only -- an extern c fn's parameters and result, a
# function pointer's signature, a pointee, a layout field -- and stay two
# types (Ptr[ffi::c::Float] is no Ptr[ffi::c::Double]). A float or double
# entering Botlish is a Float64 only when it is finite: a NaN or an infinity
# is NonFiniteFloat (the call, or the layout read, declares it), and
# negative zero is zero. Where a value type is written, ffi::c::Double means
# Float64 and ffi::c::Float is an error: Botlish has no binary32 number. C
# long double has no type.
#
# Pointers (intrinsic types and operations of this namespace):
#
#   ffi::c::Void              the C void: an incomplete pointee, no values
#   ffi::c::Ptr[T]            a C data pointer whose pointee identity is T:
#                             ffi::c::Void, a C integer scalar, ffi::c::Bool,
#                             ffi::c::Float or ffi::c::Double, an opaque
#                             struct (an incomplete C tag's identity), or
#                             another pointer
#   ffi::c::FnPtr[Fn{args: [A, ...], return: R}]
#                             a C function pointer (A and R C ABI types, R
#                             unit for a void result)
#
#   ffi::c::null[T]()         Ptr[T]      a null pointer
#   ffi::c::null?(p)          bool        whether p is null (p.null?())
#   ffi::c::clone(p)          p's type    a second alias of p (p.clone())
#   ffi::c::erase(p)          Ptr[Void]   the same address as void *
#   ffi::c::retype[T](p)      Ptr[T]      the same address of a Ptr[Void]
#
# A pointer is affine: binding, passing, returning or storing it moves it,
# and `p.clone()` is the one way to have two aliases. Affinity is not
# ownership: a pointer owns, frees and retains nothing, and is never
# dereferenced here. It is not an Int: no arithmetic, ordering, equality or
# integer conversion exists, in either direction.
#
# Temporary byte exposure (C-POINTER-BORROWING.md). A C pointer parameter of
# a foreign call may admit existing Botlish byte storage for that one call --
# never as a pointer value -- when the call's contract says, for that
# parameter, that C does not keep the pointer (noescape) and whether C reads
# or writes through it, and its pointee is a byte-sized C type
# (ffi::c::Char, SChar, UChar) or ffi::c::Void:
#
#   abi::bytes::Bytes          read access only: C sees the bytes
#   abi::bytes::MutableBytes   read: its bytes as they are; write: a private
#                              copy C may modify, which the call returns as
#                              the parameter's updated value (the caller's
#                              own value never changes: a MutableBytes is a
#                              value)
#
# A parameter that may keep the pointer, or about which nothing is known,
# never admits it: `const` is not noescape, and unknown is not
# "probably synchronous". No operation of this namespace (or any other)
# makes a Ptr of byte storage, so no pointer into Botlish storage can be
# stored, returned, captured, cloned, erased or retyped. The exposure adds
# no length and no terminator: the caller passes the count, a C integer
# scalar, separately. There is no source syntax for a contract yet: the
# facts are a foreign call's parameter metadata (a native's, and later an
# `extern c fn` declaration's).
